WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.802
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:07.990
YUFENG GUO: Morning.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:13.630
My name is Yufeng Guo, and I
host a video series on YouTube

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:18.170
called AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.070
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:26.000
I wanted to bring a little piece
of that, here today, in person.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.630
So let's go on an AI
adventure together.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.400
For the purposes
of this session,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.810
let's start with just a short
definition of machine learning,

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.140
and use that to
build up a workflow

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.890
and get into the tools.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.700
We'll then, use
that today to help

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.120
us see how we can solve
machine-learning problems,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.460
and see how we can use that
to apply to your problems

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.600
outside of I/O.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.690
And so, we'll say
that machine learning

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.780
is programming with data.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.070
And we'll use that data to
create a system or model which

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.950
is trained to do some task.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.030
And we train this model,
very importantly, using data.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.340
And so, everything
comes back to the data.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.460
So that's really the
first step of machine

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:21.250
learning-- gathering your data.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.100
It all centers and
starts from there.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.400
Next, there's typically
some kind of preparation

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.570
needed of that data.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:32.237
The raw data is often
not so suitable for use.

00:01:32.237 --> 00:01:33.820
And so, we need to
prepare it a little

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.010
before it's ready for
your machine learning.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.680
Third, we'll need
to choose a model

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.540
we want to use, then,
train it up, and evaluate

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.380
its performance, do
some fine tuning,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.400
and finally, make those
predictions-- do those tasks.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.500
So we'll go through each
of these seven steps

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.200
in detail, over the
course of this session.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.500
So first, let's
see what question

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:05.060
we are trying to use our model
to address in the first place.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.470
We'll walk through a
simple example dataset,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.940
and go through these seven steps
using the task to categorize

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.000
some data about animals.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.760
We're going to say, what
kind of animal is this,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.050
based on some input data.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:20.840
Is it a bird?

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.580
Is it a mammal?

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.574
Is it a reptile fish,
or amphibian, a bug,

00:02:24.574 --> 00:02:25.240
or invertebrate?

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.100
One of these seven types.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.090
And it will be based on a
variety of different stats

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:34.600
about a given animal.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.650
So our model will take in this
data, this structured data,

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.640
and use that to try to make a
prediction-- one of these seven

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.316
types of animals.

00:02:44.316 --> 00:02:45.940
Now that we know what
kind of situation

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.160
we're modeling,
let's dive into each

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.710
of these steps in more detail.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.330
First up, is gathering data.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.710
Just because we
know what problem

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.690
we are trying to solve
for, it doesn't always

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.870
mean that we have the
data needed to solve it.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:07.060
And without data, your
model is going nowhere.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:14.570
So ask yourself this, where does
my data live, in what format,

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.810
and how can I get to it?

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.890
These are oftentimes the
early stumbling blocks

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:26.950
as we try to use data to solve
and address our problems.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.789
And perhaps, you're
actually in a situation

00:03:28.789 --> 00:03:30.580
where you don't have
all the data you need.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.750
You need to collect
your own data.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.080
This is also a common use case.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.500
And you can get creative
in solving this problem

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.290
by building some systems
for data collection,

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.900
perhaps you make it a game.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.010
One really interesting
example of this is Quickdraw.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.890
Quickdraw is an
online game that lets

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.989
you draw a specific
picture and tries

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:54.280
to predict what you're drawing.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.090
So it tells you draw a
basketball, or draw a boat.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.770
And you know, it's kind of fun.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.070
People like it.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.730
And as a result, the
game has generated

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.590
over 1 billion doodles
of hand-drawn images

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.340
from around the world.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.260
And this dataset is
open sourced on GitHub.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.810
And there's actually an AI
Adventures episode that we just

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.899
put out if you want to learn
more about using this dataset

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:21.410
and playing with that.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.260
But back to our steps.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.640
We've gathered our data.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.240
What's next?

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.030
Well, we've got to
prepare that data.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.110
Data preparation could be its
own separate talk entirely.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.080
So we'll just touch
on a few aspects here.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.310
Explore your data before you do
any kind of machine learning.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.665
It can really help you
understand your data better,

00:04:41.665 --> 00:04:44.290
and this is fundamental
because it gives you

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.720
this intuition
about your data set,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.080
and can save you a ton
of time and headache

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.100
later on in the process.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.030
And so, I really encourage
folks to clean their data,

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.940
to look at the data, and kind
of get some intuition about it.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.610
It helps you identify
gaps, and you'll

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.610
learn a lot about what you're
working with along the way.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.580
And maybe you'll find that
you actually need more data

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.560
or that certain pieces
of data you collected

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.800
weren't actually so
useful after all.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.380
One tool I like to play with
to do this is called Facets.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.970
We'll take a little brief
detour and talk about that.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:21.460
Facets is an open source
data-visualization

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.230
tool from Google
Research, and it

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:28.210
can help you get a better sense
of your data distribution.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.420
And yes, there are lots
of tools that enable

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.790
you to do very similar things.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.070
You can write out commands
and call functions,

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.130
but this one puts
it all in one place.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:42.150
And because it's a
UI, you won't forget

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.620
to compute a
particular statistic

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.574
or look into some
corner of the data

00:05:45.574 --> 00:05:46.990
because it's all
computed for you.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:48.330
It just shows up.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.160
And so, you can just
kind of browse it.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.430
And so here, we can see
Facets applied to our data set

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:57.450
of these animals from
the zoo, and most of them

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.550
are kind of ones and zero
data sets of does it breathe?

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.394
Does it have a tail?

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:02.310
Does it have feathers?

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:03.520
Things like that.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.290
And so that's why we see this
kind of funny distribution on 0

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:07.980
to 1.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.210
But a lot of times, with
numerical distributions,

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.380
you'll see something
far more interesting.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.930
Now, let's move on to
our third step of machine

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:19.650
learning-- choosing a model.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:23.040
And we'll discuss a little
bit about model selection,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.320
how we might go about
doing this using

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.250
the least effort possible.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.360
And first, let's build
an intuition around

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.120
what is happening when a
machine-learning model is

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.390
working well.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.219
So here, we see how
the model is trying

00:06:38.219 --> 00:06:40.260
to divide up a region,
where we're trying to say,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.240
which side is blue,
which side is orange,

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.090
to match the data,
which are the dots.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.030
So it's not the hardest
example in the world.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:47.530
Right?

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.920
Everything's already kind
of separated forever--

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:50.880
separated out.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.130
But let's look at something
a little more interesting.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.560
Here, we see the model
struggle for a little bit

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.320
as it tries to find a way to
draw that enclosing circle

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.610
around the blue dots.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.580
And it's really neat to
see that these tools are

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.640
available to you today, which
are sophisticated enough

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.610
to handle all of
the minutia for you.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.310
So you don't really need to
worry about the details of how

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.250
it's getting about it.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:15.600
It's just a matter of is
it achieving the task.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.820
But it is fun to see.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.970
And different models can
model different kinds of data,

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.800
and different degrees
of complexity.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.700
So that's what model
selection is really about.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.300
It's about seeing what
model is the right fit

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.210
for your particular data set,
and the particular problem

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.870
you're trying to solve.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:36.960
Now concretely, what
we are going to do

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.140
is use TensorFlow
to achieve this.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.430
So there's lots of
talks about TensorFlow

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:46.560
here at I/O this year, so
I won't rehash those bits.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.480
But we'll see an
example, perhaps,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.810
of what it might look like
to use TensorFlow to do model

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:54.390
selection, to actually
write that code, especially

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.460
if you haven't had too
much hands on experience

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.200
with TensorFlow,
this may be kind

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:02.925
of a useful look at just
how accessible it can be.

00:08:02.925 --> 00:08:04.800
So this is an example
of a linear classifier.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.591
It's similar to the
first animation we saw.

00:08:06.591 --> 00:08:11.870
It's good for a drawing a
linear line between two spaces.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.960
And we can see that it's
pretty straightforward.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.450
But what if we had
more complex data?

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:21.980
This one-line call, well, it
kind of stays a one-line call

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.960
when you try to try it
out on a different model.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.130
So we're going to replace this
with a deep neural network,

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.710
and it only takes
a few small tweaks.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.490
And in this case, we just have
to add one additional argument

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:36.289
called hidden units.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:40.470
This defines the
structure of our network.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:44.120
In this case, we have
three layers with 30, 20,

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.610
and 10 neurons in them.

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:48.560
And so, all we needed
to do is, literally,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.150
just change the name--

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.860
just replace that text,
and add one argument.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.790
You don't have to replumb
your entire system.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.170
You don't have to delete
everything and start

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:58.110
from scratch.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.240
So that's really neat.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.000
And it's similar for
other models you can try.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.872
A lot of them just involve
one flip of the model--

00:09:05.872 --> 00:09:07.580
of the method call,
and you can basically

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:12.800
play around with these
different menu of choices.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.110
And so, in that
paradigm it allows

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:18.860
you to not have to commit
to a model beforehand.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.306
You can try one out, see how
it does, and then come back.

00:09:22.306 --> 00:09:23.930
So these seven steps,
you work through,

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.270
and you can iterate back.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:28.170
You can always go back
and cycle through.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.350
And so, that brings
us to our next step.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:31.840
We've gathered our data.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:33.500
We've prepared it.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.450
We've chosen a model,
for now at least.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.380
And now, we can do the training.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.880
Training gets a
lot of attention,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.249
because in some ways, that's
where the magic happens.

00:09:44.249 --> 00:09:46.290
But in practice, it's
often very straightforward.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.260
You just let it
run, and you wait.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:51.110
So let's take a look at what
happens during the training,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.450
and then we can see it in code.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.940
Conceptually, when training
a model, we pass in our data,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:02.000
and we ask the model right away
to try to make a prediction.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.520
But the prediction
will be pretty bad

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:08.150
because our model is
initialized randomly.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:12.050
But we make this prediction,
and we take it and compare it

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:15.920
to the true answer, the
correct answer that we know.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.320
And we use that information
to update the model.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:26.690
And so, each time we go
through and update the model

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.790
with a new piece
of data as it works

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:31.010
its way through
the training set,

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.640
it allows the model to get
better and better over time

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:36.500
with each training loop.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.840
And so, that's what the
training process looks like.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.530
But in practice, when you
actually need to code it up,

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:45.490
we take our function
we created the model,

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:48.380
and we just called
dot train on it.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.620
It's, literally, one line.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:53.840
You just need to
provide a function that

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.760
gives it the training data
inputs, and that's it.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.204
It's one line.

00:10:59.204 --> 00:11:00.745
And so, once the
training completes--

00:11:00.745 --> 00:11:04.070
if you run this function, you
go off and you grab a sandwich,

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.000
and you come back, and
it's time to do evaluation.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.900
We got to evaluate our model.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.300
It's to see how accurate it is--

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:16.520
see if it's doing a good job
of performing its primary task.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.550
So we can show it some
examples of things

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.100
that we know the
correct answers to,

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.050
and the model did not see
those questions in training.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.660
It didn't see that
data in training.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:30.590
So we use this evaluation
data that we set aside

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:33.740
to measure the
performance of our model.

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.770
And conceptually, it looks
very similar to training.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:38.387
It's still this same shape.

00:11:38.387 --> 00:11:40.220
But the important
difference is that there's

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.300
no arrow at the top.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:42.920
It's not a closed loop.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.600
The model does not get updated
once we check its prediction

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:48.710
results.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.740
And so, if we did
update the model,

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.690
then it would be no
different than training,

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:55.790
and we'd be
essentially cheating.

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:59.330
So the evaluation data is
kind of precious in that way.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.610
We don't use it to
update the model.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:04.460
We hold it aside,
and we reserve it

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:09.380
for evaluating the
performance of our model.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:12.800
In code, it's just as simple
as everything else we've seen.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.450
We just call dot evaluate.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:18.170
You want to evaluate,
you call dot evaluate.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:22.025
And notice, that we are
passing in evaluation data.

00:12:22.025 --> 00:12:24.050
It's an eval input function.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.390
So it's not the training data.

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.071
And if we were using
the training data--

00:12:28.071 --> 00:12:29.570
let's say, we copied
that line down.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.610
We just swapped out,
trained for evaluate,

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.470
and we forgot to
change the data source.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.150
Now we're evaluating the
performance of our model using

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:42.200
the training data, and that
would be a big mistake,

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:47.100
because the model has optimized
itself for the training data.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:51.650
And so, if you evaluate using
that same data, what happens

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.830
is you misrepresent the true
performance of your model,

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:58.430
and it will likely perform
poorly on real-world data

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.520
once you deploy it,
since it's never seen

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.810
and you've never
measured how it does

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:06.680
with previously unseen data.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.530
So this brings us
to our next point.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.907
How can we actually
improve our model then?

00:13:14.907 --> 00:13:15.740
We ran the training.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:16.790
We did this thing.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:18.290
We ran the evaluation.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:19.130
Now what?

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:20.630
Do we run training some more?

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.460
How can we tweak it further?

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.660
We could always swap
out for a new model.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.550
But what if we've
decided on this model,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.030
but we want to improve it as is?

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:31.910
This is where hyper-parameter
tuning comes in.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.630
These models have
parameters of their own,

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:38.270
but what values should we use?

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:40.910
And how do we pick them?

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:43.800
Much like how you can
try out different models,

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:47.360
you can also try out lots of
different model parameters.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:49.640
This process called
hyper-parameter tuning,

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:53.460
in some ways, still remains
an active area of research.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:57.050
But that doesn't mean that you
can't take advantage of it.

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:00.200
Conceptually, we
take our training,

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:04.010
and instead of just training one
model, we'll tweak that model,

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.290
and create a couple
of different variants.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:08.840
And so we'll run training
on different variants

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:12.080
of the same underlying model,
but with different model

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:16.290
parameters, and see how
that affects our accuracy.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.670
So we'll train and evaluate
all of these different variants

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:22.520
of the same, or similar,
models, and then

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:23.960
see which one performs best.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:27.060
That's how it informs
our parameter choice.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.270
But you see, a lot of
that is experimental.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:32.330
You've got to try them
to see what works.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.390
So you might have to
break out your four loop.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:38.210
And so, Yeah, the task of
choosing good hyper-parameters

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.799
is something, perhaps
you've intuited this,

00:14:41.799 --> 00:14:44.090
you could probably turn into
a machine-learning problem

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:45.140
itself--

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.270
optimizing the
parameters for the model

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:49.196
that you're using
for machine learning.

00:14:49.196 --> 00:14:51.070
But we'll have to save
that for another talk.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.040
So we've gathered data.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.380
We've prepared it, chosen
a model and trained it,

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.620
evaluated the data, and we
tuned the hyper-parameters.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.119
At long last, we have
reached our last step,

00:15:04.119 --> 00:15:05.160
making those predictions.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:08.020
This is the whole
point of training up

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:09.480
the model in the first place.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.850
It wasn't just to get
a really good accuracy,

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:15.810
or to create heat on our server.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:19.860
It was to actually do
something useful with it.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:23.610
And so, making predictions,
we take the model

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:26.910
and we deploy it somewhere,
or we isolate it out,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.120
and we can show the model
some data that it hasn't

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.180
been trained on, and then
see what kind of outputs

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:36.320
it predicts.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.120
And so, that's our seven steps--

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.330
conceptual seven steps
of machine learning.

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.784
But let's see what it actually
looks like in practice.

00:15:46.784 --> 00:15:48.450
We'll turn to the
tooling side of things

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:51.960
and look at what you use,
beyond just TensorFlow,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.240
and how you might actually
achieve these seven steps.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:58.560
So we're going to go
and look at some code,

