back to the slides

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:31.560
briefly, and take a
look at another tool.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:36.270
Aside from CO Lab,
there is another tool

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:38.520
that we have that's
very similar.

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:42.130
It's also notebook based, and
you might have heard of it.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.610
It's called Kaggle.

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:48.420
And while Kaggle is most
known for its competitions

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.810
or discussion forums
and data sets,

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.510
it also has a feature
called Kernels.

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:57.480
And Kernels is really just
a fancy name for notebook.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:59.460
And the Kernels look
something like this.

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.160
This might start to look
familiar from Co Lab,

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.560
except it's blue.

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:10.330
And Kernels is different
in a couple of subtle ways.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:11.540
Firstly, I want to--

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:15.030
let's switch over to the
demo for Kaggle Kernels

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:17.600
and we'll see what
that looks like.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:19.660
How's that?

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:22.350
So I took the notebook that
we had earlier in Co Lab,

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:24.840
and I downloaded
the notebook itself

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:26.790
as an IPython notebook file.

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:30.720
And since Kaggle has
data sets, we actually

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:33.930
have a zoo animal
classification dataset.

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:36.960
How convenient that I
chose the exact dataset

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:39.060
that already exists on Kaggle.

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:41.620
And so, we can click
New Kernel, and I'm

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.350
going to choose notebook.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.660
And Kaggle Kernels not
only run in Python,

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:49.320
but you can also choose to run
them in R, for those of you who

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:54.330
prefer R. And what I
can do is I can actually

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:57.690
upload a notebook--

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:00.100
well, first I need to
download that notebook.

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:01.840
So let's do that.

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:05.760
So from Co Lab,
we will download--

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.610
this is what I get
for zooming in a lot--

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:12.740
download the book.

00:27:12.740 --> 00:27:14.330
And then, once
it's downloaded, we

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:19.150
can upload that notebook
back into Kaggle Kernels.

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:20.060
And so-- boom.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.889
We have that same
notebook now in Kernels.

00:27:22.889 --> 00:27:23.930
There is one small tweak.

00:27:23.930 --> 00:27:28.430
Because we had to upload a
file from the local drive,

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:30.750
for Co Lab, we're going
to get rid of that.

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:34.040
And the only other tweak is that
it in Kaggle Kernels, the data

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:37.080
lives in one directory
up called input.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:41.380
So if I run the first cell, and
then we'll work our way down,

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:44.840
we'll see that the
data lives in input,

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:46.177
and it's the same kind of thing.

00:27:46.177 --> 00:27:48.260
And since we already saw
the rest of the notebook,

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:50.450
it's, literally, the same thing.

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:54.860
What is interesting, though,
is that in kernels, we can--

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:56.810
let's see-- let's
give it a name.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.360
And we can commit that notebook.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:07.430
And what Kaggle
Kernels does is it

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:12.710
will run your notebook in
a new fresh environment,

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:15.680
separate from the
session that you're in.

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:18.350
This will generate a notebook
with all of the outputs

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:23.420
that we've been seeing in a kind
of nice view-only format, which

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:25.180
is really useful for sharing.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:27.500
Because what if you want
to share your notebook,

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:30.260
as well as how it was
executed, to others.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:33.530
You want reproducible
notebooks, and so that's

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:34.580
kind of what we see here.

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:35.630
So we ran our notebook.

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:36.650
It's done.

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:40.130
And we can view that snapshot.

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:42.080
So it's kind of
like a GitHub model

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:44.950
of like you can commit
versions of your notebook.

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:48.770
And so, by default, your
notebooks are private.

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:53.510
And we can see here that things
ran, the outputs are shown,

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:56.480
and I didn't have to go through
and run these one by one.

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:59.690
And if I ran these cells
out of order, manually,

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:02.270
earlier, to yield
my results, running

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:03.890
them top to bottom
like this will

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:05.930
help catch those
kinds of bugs that

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.840
would help with the--
make it hard to reproduce

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:12.140
my performance down the
road, when I come back

00:29:12.140 --> 00:29:15.390
to this next week,
or next month.

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:20.430
And if you share your notebooks,
you can then fork them.

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:21.310
You can fork them.

00:29:21.310 --> 00:29:23.360
You can also get others
to fork your notebooks,

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:26.460
as well as add collaborators
to your notebooks.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:29.870
You can add users to join
you on the same notebook,

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:31.590
rather than just forking them.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.650
So there's a lot of great
collaboration models

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:36.020
across Co Lab and
Kaggle, depending

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:38.530
on your particular use case.

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:42.680
And, I guess, it would be good
to also just briefly mention

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:51.290
that like Co Lab, we can also
enable GPUs in Kaggle Kernels

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:51.870
as well.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:55.550
So don't let that be a
deciding factor for you.

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:57.080
So that's Kaggle Kernels.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:58.820
Let's switch back
over to the slides

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:02.300
and talk about just a few
other little tools and tips

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:05.440
and tricks, before
we wrap things up.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.920
And if we could switch
back to the slides--

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.310
awesome.

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:15.880
So let's say, you
don't want notebooks.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:19.570
Let's say, you try notebooks
and they just weren't for you,

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.820
or you have a bigger workload.

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:24.980
You need to run a
long running job.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:26.620
It's got to run for hours.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.240
Or you have a
really big data set,

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.310
and it doesn't fit
in Kaggle Kernels,

00:30:30.310 --> 00:30:32.080
or it doesn't fit on
your local directory,

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:35.920
so you're not going to
upload it to drive manually.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:39.100
Then, you can use something like
Cloud Machine Learning Engine

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:42.970
where you can kick off a job
to run-- a long running job--

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:46.830
that maybe runs across a
set of distributed machines,

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.900
all of them potentially
with GPUs attached.

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:54.100
And once you're done, you
might want to serve your model.

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:55.570
You want to make
those predictions.

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:57.762
But perhaps, you want
to do it at scale.

00:30:57.762 --> 00:30:58.720
You're building an app.

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:00.428
You train a model,
and you want to create

00:31:00.428 --> 00:31:04.870
a rest endpoint that can serve
predictions to the world.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:07.360
And so, machine-learning
engine also

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:11.410
includes an auto-scaling
prediction service.

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:14.770
You can, literally, just
take your TensorFlow model,

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:18.190
give it a name, and be done--

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.560
literally point to the
file and give it a name.

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:22.480
There aren't any other
steps, because you

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:25.580
can't do anything else, in terms
of creating a model to serve,

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:27.400
which is really neat.

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:30.010
And if you are working with
things like scikit-learn

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:32.950
or [INAUDIBLE] boost, and you
want to serve those as well,

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.360
we'll take those too.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:36.580
And so, then you don't
have to deal with the ops

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:40.720
aspect of machine learning, you
can just play with your data,

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:45.730
tweak your model, train it,
and deploy it with ease.

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:47.290
And I think most--

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.360
not most, but many of you--

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:53.590
are also perhaps aware of
the machine-learning APIs

00:31:53.590 --> 00:31:55.040
that are also available.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.880
These are pretrained APIs
for doing various tasks.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:00.250
They're a little more canned.

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:02.380
You don't provide
your own data, but it

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:05.344
does mean that everything
just works out of the box.

00:32:05.344 --> 00:32:07.510
You need something-- you
need a picture to be turned

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:11.040
into a description, you need
audio to be turned into text,

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:11.654
it just works.

00:32:11.654 --> 00:32:12.820
And so, that's kind of nice.

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:16.150
But the limitations, of course,
are that you can't customize it

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:18.220
for your specific use case.

00:32:18.220 --> 00:32:20.920
That is still something
you'll have to just wait

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:22.660
a little bit longer for.

00:32:22.660 --> 00:32:25.130
With the one notable
exception of Vision.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:30.460
So the auto ML, Vision is
available right now in alpha,

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:34.040
and so you can apply for
that, and supply your own data

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:37.820
sets to train and customize
your own Vision API.

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:40.800
So that's kind of like
a neat side tangent.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:42.700
The animations that
we saw earlier,

00:32:42.700 --> 00:32:46.840
that I showed, with the
orange and blue dots

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:49.000
came from the
TensorFlow Playground.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:52.270
So if you want to play
around with a neural network

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.000
in your browser and just
like toggle things on and off

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:59.020
and just mess around, head over
to Playground.TensorFlow.org

00:32:59.020 --> 00:33:03.010
and you can do that right there.

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:05.380
And don't worry,
you can't break it.

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:06.780
It's just through browser.

00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:10.420
You can only break
your own machine.

00:33:10.420 --> 00:33:12.870
And so what's next?

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.010
The code that we just saw is--

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:16.090
I made it public.

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:16.990
It's on Kaggle.

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:21.289
So it's at
Kaggle.com/yufeng/zoo-demo.

00:33:21.289 --> 00:33:23.080
If you want to learn
more about TensorFlow,

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.930
head on over to TensorFlow.org.

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:28.930
And thirdly, there is a
machine-learning crash course

00:33:28.930 --> 00:33:30.610
that Google released recently.

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:33.077
And so, if you want
to learn-- really dive

00:33:33.077 --> 00:33:34.660
into the concepts
of machine learning,

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:37.060
go further than what we've
talked about here today,

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:37.990
head over there.

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:40.090
There's basically
a whole curriculum

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:44.740
of videos and assignments
that you can do, and really

00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:47.377
build up your
machine-learning knowledge.

00:33:47.377 --> 00:33:49.460
And finally, if you're
interested in doing machine

00:33:49.460 --> 00:33:51.550
learning in the cloud,
you can head over

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.130
to the Cloud dome,
the white tent

00:33:54.130 --> 00:33:56.290
next to the Google
Assistant dome.

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:59.680
Or head on over to
cloud.Google.com/machinelearning

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:01.690
or /ml.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.270
As I mentioned at the
beginning, I host a video series

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.730
by the same name as the
session, AI Adventures.

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:08.739
We explore some
interesting nugget

00:34:08.739 --> 00:34:10.719
in each episode about
machine learning,

00:34:10.719 --> 00:34:13.330
and try to do some hands-on
demos once in a while,

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:16.600
and do some interviews
with interesting folks.

00:34:16.600 --> 00:34:20.830
So hopefully you'll check
it out and subscribe.

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:23.469
So I want to thank you for
joining me in this session,

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:24.639
and we really--

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:27.550
you know, I'd really appreciate
feedback on the session,

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:30.280
information, so please head
on over to the schedule,

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:33.800
and you can log in and
give some feedback.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:34.704
Thanks a lot.

00:34:34.704 --> 00:34:37.174
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:37.174 --> 00:34:41.882
[MUSIC PLAYING]

